{:edb/db-before-t 256 :edb/db-after-t 257 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533569 50 #inst "2026-10-11T09:48:31.466Z" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151832 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $24 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$\n\n- $17 \\equiv 21 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $19 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151832 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $24 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$\n2. $17 \\equiv 21 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n3. $19 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151832 1074 "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3 \\mid (24-12)$. There is no remainder:\n$(24-12) \\div 3 = 12 \\div 3 = 4$\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4 \\mid (17-21)$. There is no remainder:\n$(17-21) \\div 4 = -4 \\div 4 = -1$\n- Case III gives a false statement because $9 \\not\\mid (19-12)$. Notice that $19-12 = 7$, which is not divisible by $9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151832 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3 \\mid (24-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-12) \\div 3 = 12 \\div 3 = 4\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4 \\mid (17-21).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(17-21) \\div 4 = -4 \\div 4 = -1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $9 \\not\\mid (19-12).$ Notice that $19-12 = 7,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151833 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-11 \\equiv -29 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $90 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$\n\n- $-10 \\equiv 4 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151833 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $-11 \\equiv -29 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n2. $90 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$\n3. $-10 \\equiv 4 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151833 1074 "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $6 \\mid (-11-(-29))$. There is no remainder:\n$(-11-(-29)) \\div 6 = 18\\div 6 = 3$\n- Statement II is true because $9 \\, \\mid \\, (90-0)$. There is no remainder:\n$(90-0) \\div 9 = 90\\div 9 = 10$\n- Statement III is false because $5 \\not\\mid (-10-4)$. Notice that $-10-4 = -14$, which is not divisible by $5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151833 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $6 \\mid (-11-(-29)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-11-(-29)) \\div 6 = 18\\div 6 = 3\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true because $9 \\, | \\, (90-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(90-0) \\div 9 = 90\\div 9 = 10\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $5 \\not\\mid (-10-4).$ Notice that $-10-4 = -14,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151835 1071 "Given that $-7  \\equiv 17 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=6$\n\n- $n=5$\n\n- $n=4$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186151835 1071 "Given that $-7 \\equiv 17 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n$?\n\n1. $n=6$\n2. $n=5$\n3. $n=4$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151835 1074 "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have\n\n$$\na-b = -7-17= -24\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $6 \\, \\mid \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 6 = -4$, and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6$.\n- Note that $5 \\!\\not{\\mid} \\, (-24)$. Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5$.\n- Note that $4 \\, \\mid \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 4 = -6$, and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186151835 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b = -7-17= -24.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $6 \\, | \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 6 = -4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $5 \\!\\not{|} \\, (-24).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $4 \\, | \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 4 = -6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409434 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409439 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409443 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409444 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152074 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409435 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152074 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409437 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152074 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409440 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152074 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409441 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152075 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409432 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152075 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409433 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152075 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409436 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152075 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409438 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152075 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409442 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152336 1050 420.0 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150989 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186152747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409421 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152747 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152747 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $11≡1(mod5)$\n2. $27≡15(mod7)$\n3. $30≡ - 18(mod4)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186152747 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151055 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186152758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409431 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152758 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186152758 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $19≡5(mod4)$\n2. $22≡7(mod3)$\n3. $20≡9(mod8)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186152758 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186153445 1064 "Suppose $a$ is an integer, and ${\\color{red}{n}}$ is a positive integer. As we've seen, the *division algorithm* states that there exists a unique integer $q$ and a unique positive integer ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ such that\n\n$$\na = q{\\color{red}{n}}+{\\color{blue}{r}}, \\qquad 0\\leq {\\color{blue}{r}} < {\\color{red}{n}}\n$$\n\nWhen two integers $a$ and $b$ have the *same remainder* ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{n}}$, we say they are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$, and we write\n$a\\equiv b\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **modulus**.\n\nTo demonstrate this concretely, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n1, \\qquad 6, \\qquad 11, \\qquad 16\n$$\n\nThese numbers are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol 5$ because they each have the same remainder $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 0 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n6 &= 1 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n11 &= 2 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\\\\n16 &= 3 \\cdot 5 + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ≡6 & (mod5) \\\\ 1 & ≡11 & (mod5) \\\\ 1 & ≡16 & (mod5) \\\\ 6 & ≡11 & (mod5) \\\\ 6 & ≡16 & (mod5) \\\\ 11 & ≡16 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n2, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 12, \\qquad 17\n$$\n\nThese numbers are *also* congruent modulo $5$ because they each have a remainder of $\\color{blue}{2}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 0 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n7 &= 1 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n17 &= 3 \\cdot 5 + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ≡7 & (mod5) \\\\ 2 & ≡12 & (mod5) \\\\ 2 & ≡17 & (mod5) \\\\ 7 & ≡12 & (mod5) \\\\ 7 & ≡17 & (mod5) \\\\ 12 & ≡17 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo visualize congruence modulo $5$ more generally, let's write the sequence $0, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\ldots$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $5$ numbers.\n\n![](images/24/24c9af7d731f0be479f36367ce2e1deb0d2d691ffa575cfa843a1ca1742d2698.png)\n\nEach \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $5$. Every number on a given spoke is congruent modulo $5$ to every other number on that spoke." 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186153445 1064 "Suppose $a$ is an integer, and ${\\color{red}{n}}$ is a positive integer. As we've seen, the *division algorithm* states that there exists a unique integer $q$ and a unique positive integer ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ such that\n\n$$\na = q{\\color{red}{n}}+{\\color{blue}{r}}, \\qquad 0\\leq {\\color{blue}{r}} < {\\color{red}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWhen two integers $a$ and $b$ have the *same remainder* ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{n}},$ we say they are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol n,$ and we write\n\n$$\na\\equiv b\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **modulus**.\n\nTo demonstrate this concretely, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n1, \\qquad 6, \\qquad 11, \\qquad 16\n$$\n\nThese numbers are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol 5$ because they each have the same remainder $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =0\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 6 & =1\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 11 & =2\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 16 & =3\\cdot 5+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 1 & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 1 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 6 & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 6 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 11 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n2, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 12, \\qquad 17\n$$\n\nThese numbers are *also* congruent modulo $5$ because they each have a remainder of $\\color{blue}{2}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =0\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 7 & =1\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 17 & =3\\cdot 5+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 2 & ≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 2 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 7 & ≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 7 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 12 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo visualize congruence modulo $5$ more generally, let's write the sequence $0, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\ldots$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $5$ numbers.\n\n![](images/24/24c9af7d731f0be479f36367ce2e1deb0d2d691ffa575cfa843a1ca1742d2698.png)\n\nEach \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ Every number on a given spoke is congruent modulo $5$ to every other number on that spoke." 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186153449 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've been using the fact that $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b){:}$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nTo prove this result, first note that if $a$ and $b$ have the same remainder $r$ when divided by $n$, then by the division algorithm\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= pn + r \\\\\nb &= qn + r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some integers $p$ and $q$.\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\n(a-b) = (p-q)n\n$$\n\nNow, if we denote\n\n$$\nk=p-q\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n(a-b) = kn\n$$\n\nSo, $(a-b)$ is an integer multiple of $n$, which means that $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$.\n\nConversely, assume that $n \\mid (a-b)$. Then there is an integer $k$ such that $a-b= nk$.\n\nNow, by Euclid's division algorithm, there exist unique integers $m$ and $r\\in \\{0, 1, \\dots n-1\\}$ such that\n\n$$\nb={\\color{blue} n}m+\\color{red}r\n$$\n\nNote that this means that $b \\, \\cong \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$. So, we have\n\n$$\na - b = nk \\mid ⟹a = b + nk; ⟹a = (nm + r) + nk; ⟹a = n(m + k) + r\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$ and $b \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$, so $a \\, \\equiv \\, b \\, \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\, n)$.\n\nThis proves that if $n$ divides $(a-b)$ then $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$." 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186153449 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've been using the fact that $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b){:}$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nTo prove this result, first note that if $a$ and $b$ have the same remainder $r$ when divided by $n,$ then by the division algorithm\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =pn+r \\\\ b & =qn+r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some integers $p$ and $q.$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\n(a-b) = (p-q)n.\n$$\n\nNow, if we denote $k=p-q\\in\\mathbb Z,$ then\n\n$$\n(a-b) = kn.\n$$\n\nSo, $(a-b)$ is an integer multiple of $n,$ which means that $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n.$ \n\nConversely, assume that $n \\mid (a-b)$. Then there is an integer $k$ such that $a-b= nk.$ \n\nNow, by Euclid's division algorithm, there exist unique integers $m$ and $r\\in \\{0, 1, \\dots n-1\\}$ such that $b={\\color{blue} n}m+\\color{red}r.$ Note that this means that $b \\,  \\cong \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b=nk & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=b+nk \\\\  & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=(nm+r)+nk \\\\  & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=n(m+k)+r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,  $a \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n)$ and $b \\,  \\equiv \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n),$ so $a \\, \\equiv \\, b  \\, \\,  (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n).$\n\nThis proves that if  $n$ divides $(a-b)$ then $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n.$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186153471 1064 "In general, two integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b)$.\n$a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ because $5\\,\\mid\\, (6-1)$. There is no remainder:\n$(6-1)\\div 5 = 5\\div 5 = 1$\n- $-2\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$ because $6\\,\\mid\\, \\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)$. There is no remainder:\n$\\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)\\div 6 = 6\\div 6 = 1$\n\nHowever, the following numbers are not congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\not\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because $4\\,\\not\\mid\\, (6-1)$. Note that $(6-1) = 5$, which is not divisible by $4$.\n- $0\\not\\equiv 13\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,12)$ because $12\\,\\not\\mid\\, (0-13)$. Note that $(0-13) = -13$, which is not divisible by $12$." 13194139533569 17592186045417 false] [17592186153471 1064 "In general, two integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b).$ \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ because $5\\,\\mid\\, (6-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-1)\\div 5 = 5\\div 5 = 1\n$$\n\n- $-2\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$ because $6\\,\\mid\\, \\left((-2)-(-8)\\right).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n\\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)\\div 6 = 6\\div 6 = 1\n$$\n\nHowever, the following numbers are not congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\not\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because $4\\,\\not\\mid\\, (6-1).$ Note that $(6-1) = 5,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- $0\\not\\equiv 13\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,12)$ because $12\\,\\not\\mid\\, (0-13).$ Note that $(0-13) = -13,$ which is not divisible by $12.$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409342 1080 "II only" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409342 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409342 1083 #uuid "02e87fd7-2