409425 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409432 1069 "q-76964" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409432 1071 "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=9$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=3$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409432 1072 #uuid "0ce0c9ed-8f49-5909-b44d-93bd60aa3e7a" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409432 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409426 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1069 "q-103390" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1071 "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=9$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1072 #uuid "1c433443-9a9c-5d0b-84b5-bc247b100915" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409433 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409424 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1069 "q-103385" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1072 #uuid "2a4a4ae5-eba9-597a-b12c-2ce503fbc86a" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409434 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409428 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1069 "q-76953" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $17≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $23≡-10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1072 #uuid "42227076-6750-5c0c-9261-152604f91699" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409435 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409417 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1069 "q-76972" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1071 "Given that $8≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=5$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1072 #uuid "80a88f89-ac3c-581b-83f4-0bc0a136d398" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409436 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=8-23=-15.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷3=-5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $4∤(-15).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $5∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷5=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409427 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1069 "q-212516" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n-3≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $-18$\n- $-15$\n- $-9$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1072 #uuid "97d3bcb5-c2f9-55ac-a2c8-208f62457770" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409437 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-18)).$ Notice that $-3-(-18)=15,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $12∣(-3-(-15)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-3-(-15))÷12=12÷12=1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-9)).$ Notice that $-3-(-9)=6,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$-15$ only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409423 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1069 "q-103490" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1071 "Given that $13≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1072 #uuid "9a58d29a-b4cc-50fc-9d69-711422d71682" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409438 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=13-(-5)=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $6∣18$ because $18÷6=3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409429 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1069 "q-113658" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n11≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $3$\n- $7$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1072 #uuid "aa9fc443-6ba1-5837-ae0a-5b71ce84408d" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409439 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $4,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $4$ numbers. \n\n![](images/06/0644cdd579eeaa9168808434167e1e007a698efeab2e5d031bf8b0159a245af4.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $4.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $4∤(11-1).$ Notice that $11-1=10,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-3).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-3)÷4=8÷4=2\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-7)÷4=4÷4=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ and $7$ only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409430 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1069 "q-76958" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $12≡36\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $-9≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $21≡45\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1072 #uuid "ae89ee49-3f2d-51e4-a2f6-ac3e918a674e" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409440 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $2∣(12-36).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(12-36)÷2=-24÷2=-12\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $4∤(-9-18).$ Notice that $-9-18=-27,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $8\\,|\\,(21-45).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(21-45)÷8=-24÷8=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409422 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1069 "q-76959" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $-7≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $6≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1072 #uuid "b17c24c5-e750-5126-8785-6318018ca783" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409441 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409420 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1069 "q-103387" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1071 "Given that $-3≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1072 #uuid "e77d765e-3b79-5534-bec0-79f592cc8d1e" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409442 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-3-15=-18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷2=-9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷6=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷9=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409419 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1069 "q-113657" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n6≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $0$\n- $1$\n- $2$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1072 #uuid "ff47ebb7-b97b-504b-85d7-8f24ec1bf6ea" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409443 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $2,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $2$ numbers. \n\n![](images/93/93c588ff95bd8987e60ce2221ebef6e44c05039c13608ba35968a28c8582dd34.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $2.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-0)÷2=6÷2=3\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $2∤(6-1).$ Notice that $6-1=5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-2).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-2)÷2=4÷2=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$0$ and $2$ only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409418 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1069 "q-113654" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n7≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $2$\n- $10$" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1072 #uuid "fffccd1b-296a-5eb0-9657-b20162f80bc6" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true] [17592186409444 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $3,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $3$ numbers. \n\n![](images/46/46c64b99bf297e841b79bcc2e97c8a650b6ae94af8e11f2d4e85f2e8513def6c.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $3.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-1)÷3=6÷3=2\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $3∤(7-2).$ Notice that $7-2=5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-10).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-10)÷3=-3÷3=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$1$ and $10$ only.\"" 13194139533569 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" 17592186409342 "answer-0385a2d3-ff26-5002-954b-401ff5ea7fe4" 17592186409343 "answer-05752e2f-49d8-5029-8640-38d4fb2c4302" 17592186409344 "answer-07c58a97-2d6f-5dcb-84fe-89a84ea632bb" 17592186409345 "answer-0e65da69-f3b4-5682-a2d1-fed159b1cca9" 17592186409346 "answer-12a59032-d154-5cd1-9f78-1e77115fceb4" 17592186409347 "answer-1762b1ce-d004-57e6-ae76-4ab71ee3f948" 17592186409348 "answer-1a91b125-6713-5078-ad8a-07ca9759bd03" 17592186409349 "answer-1c2570f7-17be-5eab-8bd6-c0a17a4ce8fb" 17592186409350 "answer-1e6491d9-1d90-5386-995a-284889bdd49e" 17592186409351 "answer-1eba9774-714c-562d-9d13-0447aea71267" 17592186409352 "answer-1fe10586-c932-5ec9-a06b-8cf711a3abc4" 17592186409353 "answer-283ae9e3-5cbe-51dc-93f9-66705b94a4b5" 17592186409354 "answer-28a23adb-0758-5db2-acbc-ce4a08c7bea5" 17592186409355 "answer-297ef0c5-c965-585b-8851-1eee6d2a0890" 17592186409356 "answer-2b860813-d4b1-5fef-b506-6ffcc8c555af" 17592186409357 "answer-2b9ba739-5c28-5602-a51b-7adc5826aea5" 17592186409358 "answer-2e4173a3-d315-5fb7-9f00-3b